.TH ENV 1 2026-09-28 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
env \- run a program in a modified environment
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B env
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-u
.IR name ]...
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ name = value ]...
.RI [ utility
.RI [ argument ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B env
builds an environment from its own, with the changes the options and
operands ask for, and runs
.I utility
with the given
.I arguments
in it.
.I utility
is searched for in the
.B PATH
of the new environment, as
.BR execvp (3)
does; if the new environment has no
.BR PATH ,
.B /bin:/usr/bin
is searched.
.PP
Each
.IR name = value
operand sets
.I name
to
.IR value ,
replacing any earlier value.
The first operand that does not contain
.B =
is taken as
.IR utility ;
everything after it is passed to
.I utility
unchanged.
.PP
With no
.IR utility ,
.B env
writes the resulting environment to standard output, one
.IR name = value
per line.
.PP
The best-known use is the interpreter line
.B #!/usr/bin/env
.IR interpreter ,
which finds
.I interpreter
through
.B PATH
instead of naming its location.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are only recognized before the first
.IR name = value
operand.
.TP
.B \-i
Start from an empty environment instead of the inherited one.
.TP
.B \-
A lone hyphen is an obsolescent synonym for
.BR \-i .
.TP
.BI \-u " name"
.TQ
.BI \-u name
Remove
.I name
from the environment.
May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
If
.I utility
is run, the exit status is that of
.IR utility .
Otherwise:
.TP
.B 0
The environment was written to standard output.
.TP
.B 125
An option was invalid, an assignment had an empty name, or
.B env
itself failed.
.TP
.B 126
.I utility
was found but could not be run.
.TP
.B 127
.I utility
was not found.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Of the new environment: where
.I utility
is looked for.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a program with an extra variable:
.PP
.EX
$ env LC_ALL=C sort file
.EE
.PP
Run a program with nothing but
.BR PATH :
.PP
.EX
$ env \-i PATH=/bin:/usr/bin make
.EE
.PP
A script that runs with whichever
.B perl
comes first in
.BR PATH :
.PP
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env perl
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR execvp (3),
.BR getenv (3),
.BR environ (7)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024.
.B \-u
is an extension found in the BSDs and GNU coreutils.
